#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18541d05-49d2-5f19-b579-6b534b3bcfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y+z+w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435522} {:answer/id #uuid "1e19600e-76b4-5880-a603-02783cbb324e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2z-w+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435523} {:answer/id #uuid "36b42409-fb4f-52cb-a983-864ab8cc5f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+z-w-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435525} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f12775-7dc0-5662-9058-bbd97ad38d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y-z+w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435536} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2e0787-ad3a-5c7f-add5-4e016dc6c7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-3z+w+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e19600e-76b4-5880-a603-02783cbb324e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-2z-w+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435523} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b834ec-93e3-593b-87ef-065fbe59d9c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b798538-8660-5c1f-9d93-5bdad8bdb40c" :question/math-academy-id "q-129477" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=x+y-{z}^{2}$ at the point $(1,0,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y-{z}^{2})=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y-{z}^{2})=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y-{z}^{2})=-2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,0,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,0,1) & =-2(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0,1)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,0,1) \\\\ w & =(x-1)+y-2(z-1) \\\\ w & =x+y-2z+1 \\\\ x+y-2z-w+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032b951d-051b-5c7e-a89c-9ad494e6d92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435520} {:answer/id #uuid "0692ed8e-a531-5991-a793-f96a4aa13ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435521} {:answer/id #uuid "81bbb1e3-0f5e-5556-b6d5-5d35199efe2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435532} {:answer/id #uuid "af437ebd-7c23-5075-a34e-67693069d1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435535} {:answer/id #uuid "cccd4290-3c2e-59d1-86ec-25b254194b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032b951d-051b-5c7e-a89c-9ad494e6d92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435520} :answer-field/id #uuid "93540a4d-f358-5551-98bf-028208b64b0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1863d48a-c899-5d14-89ee-82ba61f0f79b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129559" :question/problem "At which point on the surface $z={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-7$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector $v=⟨6,-8,-1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-7)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-7)=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x,\\,4y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨6,-8,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2x,\\,4y,\\,-1⟩=⟨6,-8,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2cx=6 \\\\ 4cy=-8 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(1)x=6\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n4(1)y=-8\\,⟹\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=3$ and $y=-2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={3}^{2}+2(-2)^{2}-7 \\\\  & =9+8-7 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(3,-2,10).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6521128c-8b33-54da-8c83-d27bef687244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435529} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c4d22-52c4-582f-b256-0ddd6fa135ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1,-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435530} {:answer/id #uuid "84cf53e7-7029-5a30-8dd0-ad54d0eb0064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435533} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd79e5d-b439-5f83-a72f-40d75fd4cdd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1,-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435537} {:answer/id #uuid "d89ebc6c-8c25-5ee8-a102-c0421505fca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d89ebc6c-8c25-5ee8-a102-c0421505fca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435540} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1008d1e-ad25-59f1-8249-03f1bd3c30fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0b70f07-48f3-5e91-bb4d-83ea4566f9c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-129560" :question/problem "At which point on the surface $z=4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector $v=⟨-2,-5,1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2})=-4x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2})=-10y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨-4x,\\,-10y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,-5,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨-4x,\\,-10y,\\,-1⟩=⟨-2,-5,1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4cx=-2 \\\\ -10cy=-5 \\\\ -c=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-4(-1)x=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-10(-1)y=-5\\,⟹\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4-2{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-5{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4de868c0-80b7-5cbb-9dc2-ba04493471fc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016f6151-b920-51fb-9d38-ba687d166468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435519} {:answer/id #uuid "2809590b-c7a6-55d5-a41a-10d24affb73e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435524} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7f9f81-4423-520f-8c2d-93184d954cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435527} {:answer/id #uuid "60e34da5-4c5d-590d-b3f8-3cc87ff70f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435528} {:answer/id #uuid "9d343591-1c30-58e2-ad9e-f585b09e607e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016f6151-b920-51fb-9d38-ba687d166468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435519} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6ae7c04-0a9c-587c-af8d-7844f40ca12a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "986c989d-58b2-593e-8458-dc3fb233ef93" :question/math-academy-id "q-73840" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}$ at the point $(0,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(0,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,0) & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+0)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+0)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,0) & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+0)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,0)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,0) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-0)+1(y-0) \\\\ z-1 & =x+y \\\\ -x-y+z-1 & =0 \\\\ x+y-z+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37e1eeb8-4189-5718-8f50-bdf2f86033b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+15y+16z-w-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435526} {:answer/id #uuid "7147124b-3c14-59fb-b95d-12d323b0e513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+15y+16z+w+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435531} {:answer/id #uuid "df2eecc4-dc64-5c9b-8ab8-81624c44bb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-15y+16z+w+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435541} {:answer/id #uuid "f3dc19b2-fcd8-593e-b5c7-c02d25ef9070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+15y-16z+w-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435542} {:answer/id #uuid "f64cc117-a5b0-5386-a6c5-f6d385b6497f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-15y-16z+w+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7147124b-3c14-59fb-b95d-12d323b0e513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+15y+16z+w+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435531} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6aa0ef9-33dc-511a-a8b4-7535395d3236" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "923d3b9b-11dc-5749-a7e2-257ace8090ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-73849" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=2x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}z$ at the point $(2,-2,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}z)=2{y}^{2}-6xz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}z)=4xy+{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}z)=2yz-3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,-2,1) & =2(-2)^{2}-6(2)(1)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,-2,1) & =4(2)(-2)+(1)^{2}=-15 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,-2,1) & =2(-2)(1)-3(2)^{2}=-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-2 & =(x-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,-2,1)+(y+2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,-2,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,-2,1) \\\\ w-2 & =-4(x-2)-15(y+2)-16(z-1) \\\\ w-2 & =-4x+8-15y-30-16z+16 \\\\ 4x+15y+16z+w+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
